terminal.js — Functional Specification
1. Purpose

terminal.js controls ONLY the interactive terminal in the hero section.

It is responsible for:

Reading user input.
Recognizing terminal commands.
Printing terminal output.
Maintaining terminal history during the current page session.
Triggering navigation/scrolling when a command requires it.
Redirecting to GitHub when a specific project is requested.

It must NOT control:

GitHub API fetching.
Blog API fetching.
Background animations.
Logo animation.
Education/book animation.
Blog carousel behavior.
General page initialization.

Those belong to their respective modules.

2. Terminal behavior

The terminal should behave like a small interactive CLI, not like a fake static terminal.

User types:

> hello

and presses Enter.

The command is processed and the output is appended below it.

The terminal should retain previous commands and their output, so the interaction feels like an actual shell.

Example:

> hello

Hello! I'm Krishnang Pandey...
...

> projects

Available projects:
...

>

The exact visual styling belongs to terminal.css, NOT terminal.js.

3. Supported commands

There are four primary command categories.

hello

Purpose: introduce Krishnang.

Input:

hello

Output should contain a concise introduction about:

Name: Krishnang Pandey
Student status
University
Degree / specialization

Do NOT make hello dump the entire portfolio.

It should be an introduction, not a résumé.

After printing the introduction, the terminal remains active.

It does NOT navigate anywhere.

freelance

Purpose: introduce the freelancing section.

Input:

freelance

The terminal should:

Print a concise summary of available freelancing services/packages.
Tell the user that they can view the full freelancing section.
Navigate/scroll to the freelancing section.

Example conceptual output:

> freelance

Freelancing services:
- ...
- ...
- ...

Opening freelancing...

Then the page scrolls to the freelancing section.

The actual prices/services should be stored in a configuration/data structure rather than scattered throughout command logic.

Important: terminal.js should not become the source of truth for the entire freelancing section.

Ideally:

const freelanceData = ...

or imported/shared data can contain the information.

The terminal merely displays a concise version.

projects

Purpose: show the available projects and enter project-selection mode.

Input:

projects

Output:

> projects

Available projects:

1. project-name
2. project-name
3. project-name

Type:
project <project-name>

to open a project.

The terminal should NOT immediately open a project.

projects is a discovery command.

The user then enters something such as:

> project my-project
project <project-name>

Purpose: open a specific project.

Example:

> project my-project

The terminal:

Extracts the project name.
Checks whether that project exists.
If it exists, prints a short confirmation.
Redirects the user to that project's GitHub repository.

Example:

> project my-project

Opening my-project...

Then redirect.

The project mapping should be explicit.

Conceptually:

{
    "my-project": "https://github.com/...",
    "another-project": "https://github.com/..."
}

Do NOT construct GitHub URLs dynamically from arbitrary user input.

That would make typos and nonexistent repositories problematic.

4. Unknown commands

The terminal MUST handle invalid commands gracefully.

For example:

> banana

Command not found: banana

Available commands:
hello
freelance
projects

Do not throw a JavaScript exception.

Do not break the terminal.

Do not reload the page.

Do not silently ignore the input.

5. Empty input

If the user presses Enter without typing anything:

>

Nothing should happen.

Do NOT print:

Command not found:

Do NOT add an unnecessary error message.

The terminal should simply create the next prompt.

6. Command parsing

Commands should be treated sensibly with whitespace.

These should all resolve to the same command:

hello
HELLO
 Hello
hello

At minimum:

trim leading/trailing whitespace
normalize command names to lowercase

For example:

HELLO

should behave like:

hello

For project names, be deliberate about case sensitivity.

I'd recommend project identifiers being case-insensitive too:

project AFT
project aft
PROJECT aft

can resolve to the same project.

But the displayed project name should preserve its proper capitalization.

7. Help command

I strongly recommend adding:

help

even though it wasn't part of the original four.

It should print:

Available commands:

hello       Learn about me
freelance   View freelancing information
projects    List projects
project     Open a specific project
help        Show available commands
clear       Clear terminal

This makes the terminal discoverable instead of forcing the user to know the commands beforehand.

8. clear

Also add:

clear

It should clear the visible terminal history.

It must NOT:

reload the webpage
reset other sections
reset GitHub/blog data
reset animations

It only clears the terminal's displayed history.

9. Command history

The terminal should maintain command history during the current session.

If practical, support:

ArrowUp
ArrowDown

to navigate previously entered commands.

For example:

> hello
> projects
> freelance

Pressing ↑ should cycle backward through:

freelance
projects
hello

This is optional functionality, but it would make the terminal feel significantly more authentic.

10. Prevent normal form submission

If the terminal input is inside a <form>, pressing Enter must NOT cause a normal browser form submission.

The JavaScript should intercept it and process the command itself.

There should be no:

page refresh
URL modification
accidental form submission
11. Navigation behavior

terminal.js may trigger navigation to sections, but it should NOT implement the sections themselves.

For example:

freelance

can tell the browser:

scroll to #freelancing

but it should NOT contain the freelancing section's DOM construction, styling, or animations.

Same principle applies to future commands.

Think:

terminal.js
     ↓
"Go to freelancing"
     ↓
page navigation

NOT:

terminal.js
     ↓
build entire freelancing section
     ↓
style it
     ↓
animate it
12. GitHub redirects

For:

project <name>

the GitHub URL should come from a predefined project registry.

Bad:

window.location.href =
    `https://github.com/krriisshhnnaaa/${projectName}`;

Better:

const projects = {
    project1: "https://github.com/...",
    project2: "https://github.com/..."
};

Then look up the requested project.

This prevents arbitrary input from determining the destination.

13. What terminal.js should NOT contain

This is important because otherwise terminal.js will slowly become a 900-line monster. 💀

Do NOT put:

GitHub API calls
Hygraph API calls
Blog rendering
Blog card generation
Freelancing section generation
Education/book animation
Particle systems
Background animations
Logo animation
Global page initialization
CSS
Huge amounts of static HTML

inside it.

terminal.js should essentially be:

INPUT
  ↓
PARSE COMMAND
  ↓
VALIDATE
  ↓
EXECUTE COMMAND
  ↓
OUTPUT / NAVIGATE

That's it.

14. Error handling

Every command should fail gracefully.

Possible errors:

Unknown command
Unknown project
Malformed project command
Missing project name

Example:

> project

Usage:
project <project-name>

Type "projects" to see available projects.

And:

> project imaginary-project

Project not found: imaginary-project

Type "projects" to see available projects.

No JavaScript errors should reach the user.

15. Security / robustness

Even though this is a personal portfolio, don't blindly inject user input into HTML.

Avoid doing something equivalent to:

output.innerHTML += userInput;

Instead, create DOM nodes and assign user-controlled text using textContent.

This prevents the terminal from accidentally becoming an HTML/JS injection point.

Also don't execute arbitrary commands.

The terminal is a portfolio CLI simulation, not an actual shell.

So:

rm -rf /
sudo ...
python ...
npm ...
curl ...

should simply result in:

Command not found: ...

There is no reason for this terminal to execute operating-system commands.

16. Initialization

terminal.js should expose an initialization function that main.js can call.

Conceptually:

initTerminal();

main.js owns initialization.

Therefore terminal.js should not assume that it is the application's entry point.

17. The mental model

If you're implementing this yourself, I'd keep the architecture roughly:

terminal.js

┌─────────────────────┐
│ Terminal Input      │
└──────────┬──────────┘
           ↓
┌─────────────────────┐
│ Command Parser      │
└──────────┬──────────┘
           ↓
┌─────────────────────┐
│ Command Registry    │
├─────────────────────┤
│ hello               │
│ freelance           │
│ projects            │
│ project <name>      │
│ help                │
│ clear               │
└──────────┬──────────┘
           ↓
┌─────────────────────┐
│ Command Handler     │
└──────────┬──────────┘
           ↓
     ┌─────┴─────┐
     ↓           ↓
  Output      Navigation

The big rule:

terminal.js controls the terminal. It does not control the rest of the website.
